from extras import ex

ex('puberty',"In boys, testosterone from the testes causes changes such as a deeper voice and facial hair. In girls, oestrogen from the ovaries causes changes such as breasts developing and periods starting. Both may grow quickly and get body hair.",
 [('Which hormone causes most changes in boys during puberty?','Testosterone.','Oestrogen.','Insulin.','Testosterone is made in the testes.'),
  ('Where are egg cells made?','Ovaries.','Uterus.','Testes.','Ovaries release an egg about once a month.'),
  ('Which change happens to both boys and girls?','Growth of pubic and underarm hair.','Periods start.','Voice deepens a lot.','Some changes happen to everyone.'),
  ('What are hormones?','Chemical messengers carried in the blood.','Types of cell.','Bones.','Hormones travel in the blood to target organs.')],
 [('What is puberty?','The time when a child’s body develops into an adult body.'),
  ('Name the male and female sex hormones.','Testosterone (male).~Oestrogen (female).'),
  ('Explain why puberty starts at different ages for different people.','Puberty is controlled by hormones.~Hormones start being made at different times in different bodies.~Genes, diet and health can affect the timing.')])

ex('cycle',"The menstrual cycle lasts about 28 days. Ovulation usually happens around day 14. Hormones control when the uterus lining builds up and when it breaks down.",
 [('About how long is a typical menstrual cycle?','28 days.','7 days.','1 year.','It varies, but about 28 days is typical.'),
  ('What is ovulation?','The release of an egg from an ovary.','The lining breaking down.','Sperm meeting an egg.','An egg is released about once a month.'),
  ('Why does the uterus lining thicken?','To be ready for an embryo to implant.','To store food for the mother.','To make eggs.','A thick lining can support a pregnancy.'),
  ('A period happens when…','an egg is not fertilised and the lining breaks down.','an egg is fertilised.','ovulation starts.','The lining leaves the body through the vagina.')],
 [('About which day of a 28-day cycle does ovulation happen?','Day 14.'),
  ('Describe what happens to the uterus lining during the cycle.','It thickens/builds up to prepare for pregnancy.~If no egg is fertilised, it breaks down and leaves the body (a period).'),
  ('Explain why a period stops if a woman becomes pregnant.','The embryo implants in the thick lining.~Hormones keep the lining in place.~The lining is needed to support the growing baby, so it is not lost.')])

ex('pregnancy',"The placenta lets food and oxygen pass from the mother's blood to the fetus, and waste pass back. The umbilical cord connects the fetus to the placenta. Pregnancy lasts about 40 weeks.",
 [('Where does fertilisation usually happen?','In the oviduct (fallopian tube).','In the ovary.','In the vagina.','The sperm meets the egg in the oviduct.'),
  ('What does the placenta do?','Passes food and oxygen to the fetus and removes waste.','Makes sperm.','Releases eggs.','It is the exchange organ between mother and fetus.'),
  ('About how long does a human pregnancy last?','40 weeks.','12 weeks.','2 years.','About nine months.'),
  ('Why should pregnant women avoid smoking and alcohol?','Harmful substances can cross the placenta to the fetus.','They make the baby bigger.','They stop periods.','The placenta does not block all harmful chemicals.')],
 [('What is fertilisation?','When the nucleus of a sperm joins with the nucleus of an egg.'),
  ('Name the organ that supplies the fetus with oxygen and describe how it is connected.','The placenta.~It is connected to the fetus by the umbilical cord.'),
  ('Explain why smoking during pregnancy can harm the baby.','Chemicals such as nicotine and carbon monoxide cross the placenta.~Carbon monoxide reduces the oxygen reaching the fetus.~This can slow growth and lead to a low birth weight.')])

ex('flower',"The male parts are the stamens: anther and filament. The female parts are the carpel: stigma, style and ovary. Insect-pollinated flowers have bright petals, scent and nectar; wind-pollinated flowers have feathery stigmas and lots of light pollen.",
 [('Which part of a flower makes pollen?','Anther.','Stigma.','Ovary.','The anther is part of the stamen.'),
  ('Which feature suits a wind-pollinated flower?','Feathery stigma hanging outside the flower.','Bright petals.','Sweet scent.','A feathery stigma catches pollen blowing past.'),
  ('Why do some flowers make nectar?','To attract insects.','To make seeds heavier.','To catch wind.','Insects visit for nectar and carry pollen.'),
  ('Where are the ovules found?','In the ovary.','In the anther.','On the petals.','Ovules contain the female sex cells.')],
 [('What is pollination?','The transfer of pollen from an anther to a stigma.'),
  ('Name two female parts of a flower.','Stigma / style.~Ovary / ovule.'),
  ('Explain how the features of an insect-pollinated flower help pollination.','Bright petals and scent attract insects.~Insects collect nectar and pollen sticks to them.~They carry the pollen to the sticky stigma of another flower.')])

ex('seed',"Seeds and fruits are dispersed so new plants do not compete with the parent for light, water and minerals. Methods include wind (sycamore), animals (berries, burrs), water (coconut) and explosion (pea pods).",
 [('Why are seeds dispersed away from the parent plant?','To reduce competition for light, water and minerals.','To keep them warm.','So they can be pollinated.','Young plants grow better with less competition.'),
  ('A dandelion seed has a parachute of hairs. It is dispersed by…','wind.','animals eating it.','explosion.','The hairs catch the wind.'),
  ('After fertilisation, the ovary becomes the…','fruit.','flower.','pollen.','The fruit surrounds and protects seeds.'),
  ('Which seed is dispersed by animals eating the fruit?','Blackberry.','Sycamore.','Dandelion.','Seeds pass through the animal and are dropped elsewhere.')],
 [('What develops from a fertilised ovule?','A seed.'),
  ('Describe two ways seeds are dispersed, with an example of each.','Wind, e.g. dandelion or sycamore.~Animals, e.g. berries eaten or burrs on fur / explosion, e.g. pea pods.'),
  ('Explain why a sycamore seed with wings can land further from the tree.','The wings make the seed spin as it falls.~This increases air resistance so it falls slowly.~The wind has more time to carry it further away.')])

ex('germination',"Seeds do not need light to germinate, because they use their stored food. Once leaves grow, the plant needs light for photosynthesis. You can test what seeds need by changing one condition at a time.",
 [('Which is NOT needed for most seeds to germinate?','Light.','Water.','Oxygen.','Stored food provides energy until leaves grow.'),
  ('Seeds kept in a fridge germinate slowly because…','it is too cold for enzymes to work quickly.','there is too much light.','there is too much oxygen.','Low temperature slows reactions in the seed.'),
  ('Why do germinating seeds need oxygen?','For aerobic respiration to release energy.','To make chlorophyll.','To stay dry.','Respiration releases energy for growth.'),
  ('In a germination test, the control dish should…','have all the conditions: water, air and warmth.','have no seeds.','have no water and no air.','The control shows normal germination.')],
 [('What is germination?','When a seed starts to grow.'),
  ('Plan a test to find out whether seeds need water to germinate.','Set up two dishes of seeds, one with damp cotton wool and one with dry cotton wool.~Keep all other conditions the same and compare after a few days.'),
  ('Explain why a seed can germinate in the dark but the young plant will not keep growing well without light.','The seed uses its stored food for energy at first.~Once the store runs out, the plant needs to make food by photosynthesis.~Photosynthesis needs light.')])

ex('reaction',"Signs of a chemical reaction include a colour change, fizzing, a temperature change or a precipitate forming. Chemical changes are usually hard to reverse. Word equations show the reactants and products.",
 [('Which is a chemical change?','Burning wood.','Melting ice.','Dissolving sugar.','Burning makes new substances such as ash and carbon dioxide.'),
  ('Which is a sign of a chemical reaction?','A gas being given off (fizzing).','A change in shape.','Something being cut into pieces.','Fizzing shows a new substance, a gas, is made.'),
  ('In "magnesium + oxygen → magnesium oxide", the product is…','magnesium oxide.','magnesium.','oxygen.','Products are on the right of the arrow.'),
  ('Which change is easy to reverse?','Water freezing.','Cooking an egg.','Rusting.','Freezing is a physical change.')],
 [('What is a product?','A new substance made in a chemical reaction.'),
  ('Give two observations that show a chemical reaction has happened.','Colour change / fizzing (gas given off).~Temperature change / a solid (precipitate) forms / light given out.'),
  ('Explain why melting chocolate is a physical change but toasting bread is a chemical change.','Melting chocolate does not make a new substance and can be reversed by cooling.~Toasting bread makes new substances (the brown, burnt layer).~It cannot be reversed.')])

ex('acid',"Acids in everyday life include vinegar and citrus fruits; alkalis include soap and oven cleaner. Strong acids and alkalis are corrosive, so wear goggles. Red litmus turns blue in alkali; blue litmus turns red in acid.",
 [('What colour does universal indicator turn in a strong acid?','Red.','Green.','Purple.','Strong acids have a low pH and turn it red.'),
  ('A solution has a pH of 12. It is…','alkaline.','acidic.','neutral.','Above 7 is alkaline.'),
  ('Which is acidic?','Lemon juice.','Soap.','Pure water.','Lemon juice contains citric acid.'),
  ('Blue litmus paper turns red in…','acid.','alkali.','pure water.','Acids turn blue litmus red.')],
 [('What pH is neutral?','7.'),
  ('A solution turns universal indicator dark blue. Give its approximate pH and whether it is acidic or alkaline.','About pH 10–12.~Alkaline.'),
  ('Explain why universal indicator is more useful than litmus paper.','Litmus only shows whether something is acidic or alkaline.~Universal indicator shows a range of colours.~So it shows the pH and how strongly acidic or alkaline it is.')])

ex('neutral',"Neutralisation is useful: indigestion tablets neutralise extra stomach acid, and farmers use lime to neutralise acidic soil. You can follow neutralisation with an indicator. Acid + alkali → salt + water.",
 [('What are the products when an acid reacts with an alkali?','A salt and water.','Hydrogen and oxygen.','Only water.','This is neutralisation.'),
  ('Which salt does hydrochloric acid make?','A chloride.','A sulfate.','A nitrate.','Hydrochloric acid makes chlorides.'),
  ('Why do farmers add lime to fields?','To neutralise acidic soil.','To make the soil more acidic.','To kill insects.','Many crops grow better near pH 7.'),
  ('Indigestion tablets work because they are…','bases that neutralise stomach acid.','acids.','salts that add acid.','They reduce excess acid.')],
 [('What is neutralisation?','A reaction between an acid and a base that makes a salt and water.'),
  ('Name the salt made when sodium hydroxide reacts with sulfuric acid, and the other product.','Sodium sulfate.~Water.'),
  ('Explain how you could show that an acid has been neutralised by an alkali.','Add universal indicator to the acid.~Add the alkali a little at a time, stirring.~When the indicator turns green, the solution is neutral (pH 7).')])

ex('metals',"The reactivity series lists metals from most to least reactive, for example potassium, sodium, calcium, magnesium, zinc, iron, copper, gold. Iron rusts when it reacts with oxygen and water. Gold does not react, so it stays shiny.",
 [('Which metal is most reactive?','Potassium.','Iron.','Gold.','Potassium is at the top of the reactivity series.'),
  ('What two things does iron need to rust?','Oxygen and water.','Hydrogen and salt.','Light and heat.','Rust forms when iron reacts with both.'),
  ('Why is gold used for jewellery?','It is unreactive, so it does not tarnish.','It is very reactive.','It is magnetic.','Gold stays shiny for a long time.'),
  ('Magnesium burns in oxygen to make…','magnesium oxide.','magnesium chloride.','hydrogen.','Metal + oxygen → metal oxide.')],
 [('Name the product when copper reacts with oxygen.','Copper oxide.'),
  ('Give two properties that most metals share.','Good conductors of heat and electricity.~Shiny / malleable / high melting point / strong.'),
  ('Explain two ways to stop an iron gate rusting.','Paint it / cover it in oil or grease / galvanise it with zinc.~This keeps out oxygen and water.~Without both, iron cannot rust.')])

ex('metal-acid',"The test for hydrogen is a lit splint, which goes out with a squeaky pop. Very reactive metals such as sodium are not added to acids because the reaction is dangerous. Copper does not react with dilute acids.",
 [('Which gas is made when magnesium reacts with hydrochloric acid?','Hydrogen.','Oxygen.','Carbon dioxide.','Metal + acid → salt + hydrogen.'),
  ('What is the test for hydrogen?','A lit splint gives a squeaky pop.','It relights a glowing splint.','It turns limewater milky.','Hydrogen burns rapidly with a pop.'),
  ('Which metal does NOT react with dilute acid?','Copper.','Magnesium.','Zinc.','Copper is below hydrogen in reactivity.'),
  ('Zinc + sulfuric acid → ?','Zinc sulfate + hydrogen.','Zinc chloride + water.','Zinc oxide + hydrogen.','Sulfuric acid makes sulfates.')],
 [('Name the salt made when magnesium reacts with hydrochloric acid.','Magnesium chloride.'),
  ('Complete the word equation: iron + hydrochloric acid → … + …','Iron chloride.~Hydrogen.'),
  ('Describe how you could use acid to put magnesium, zinc and iron in order of reactivity.','Add the same mass/size of each metal to the same volume and concentration of acid.~Observe or count the bubbles of hydrogen.~The more quickly it fizzes, the more reactive the metal.')])

ex('charge',"Like charges repel and opposite charges attract. A charged balloon can stick to a wall or attract small bits of paper. Static charge can build up and cause a spark when it jumps to a conductor.",
 [('Two negatively charged balloons will…','repel each other.','attract each other.','do nothing.','Like charges repel.'),
  ('When a rod becomes negative after rubbing, it has…','gained electrons.','lost electrons.','gained protons.','Only electrons move.'),
  ('A positive and a negative charge will…','attract.','repel.','cancel and disappear.','Opposite charges attract.'),
  ('Why do you sometimes get a small shock from a car door?','Static charge has built up and jumps as a spark.','The car battery is leaking.','The door is magnetic.','Charge flows to earth through you.')],
 [('What type of charge does an electron have?','Negative.'),
  ('A balloon rubbed on hair becomes negative. Explain what happened to the hair.','Electrons moved from the hair to the balloon.~The hair lost electrons, so it became positively charged.'),
  ('Explain why a charged balloon sticks to a wall.','The balloon is negatively charged.~It repels electrons in the wall, leaving the wall surface positive.~Opposite charges attract, so the balloon sticks.')])

ex('current',"In a series circuit, there is only one path, so the current is the same everywhere. In a parallel circuit, the current splits between branches. Adding more bulbs in series makes each one dimmer.",
 [('What does an ammeter measure?','Current.','Voltage.','Resistance.','Current is measured in amps.'),
  ('In a series circuit, the current is…','the same everywhere.','biggest near the battery.','zero after each bulb.','There is only one path.'),
  ('Why do bulbs in parallel stay bright when one breaks?','Each branch is a separate complete path.','The current stops everywhere.','Parallel bulbs have no current.','Other branches are still complete loops.'),
  ('What happens when you add a second bulb in series?','Both bulbs are dimmer.','Both are brighter.','Nothing changes.','The total resistance increases, so current falls.')],
 [('What is an electric current?','A flow of charge.'),
  ('A series circuit has a cell, a switch and two bulbs. Describe what happens when the switch is opened.','The circuit is broken/incomplete.~No current flows, so both bulbs go out.'),
  ('Explain why the lights in a house are wired in parallel rather than series.','In parallel each light has its own complete path.~One light can be switched off or fail without the others going out.~Each light gets the full voltage, so it is bright.')])

ex('voltage',"A voltmeter is connected in parallel across a component. Resistance is measured in ohms, Ω. A bigger resistance means a smaller current for the same voltage: current = voltage ÷ resistance.",
 [('How is a voltmeter connected?','In parallel across the component.','In series with the component.','Across the switch only.','It compares energy on each side.'),
  ('What is the unit of resistance?','Ohm (Ω).','Amp (A).','Volt (V).','Resistance is measured in ohms.'),
  ('A 6 V cell drives a current through a 3 Ω resistor. What is the current?','2 A.','18 A.','0.5 A.','I = V ÷ R = 6 ÷ 3.'),
  ('Adding more cells in series increases the…','voltage.','resistance.','number of paths.','Each cell adds its voltage.')],
 [('What unit is potential difference measured in?','Volts (V).'),
  ('A 12 V battery is connected to a 4 Ω resistor. Calculate the current.','I = V ÷ R = 12 ÷ 4.~= 3 A.'),
  ('Explain why a long thin wire has more resistance than a short thick one.','Resistance is opposition to the flow of charge.~A longer wire gives electrons further to travel through the material.~A thinner wire gives less space for electrons to flow, so there are more collisions.')])

ex('foodweb',"A food web shows many linked food chains. If one population changes, others in the web are affected. Toxic chemicals can build up along a food chain, harming top predators most.",
 [('In the chain grass → rabbit → fox, the producer is…','grass.','rabbit.','fox.','Producers make their own food.'),
  ('If foxes are removed, what will happen to rabbits at first?','Their number will increase.','Their number will decrease.','No change.','Fewer predators means more rabbits survive.'),
  ('What do arrows in a food chain show?','The direction energy (food) moves.','Which animal is biggest.','Where animals live.','The arrow points to the eater.'),
  ('A herbivore eats…','only plants.','only animals.','plants and animals.','Herbivores are primary consumers.')],
 [('What is a consumer?','A living thing that gets its food by eating other living things.'),
  ('In grass → rabbit → fox, name the primary consumer and the top predator.','Primary consumer: rabbit.~Top predator: fox.'),
  ('A disease kills most of the rabbits. Explain the effect on the grass and the foxes.','Fewer rabbits eat the grass, so the grass increases.~Foxes have less food.~So the fox population decreases (or foxes eat other prey).')])
